{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfeaSJD9buxnEa2tUK9kq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I wrote all three files, and the self-check printed `OK`. Its short smoke fit gave an ELPD-LOO of −1082.6. The model I refined scored −1084.2 and the incumbent −1072.0, but the smoke fit is too short to compare against those properly. The check doesn't test novelty against the other models or convergence. PSIS-LOO on the smoke fit was also flagged unreliable: 4.3% of trials had a high Pareto k, against a 1% tolerance.\n\n**Model refined:** `personal_alternation_ideal_streak_penalty` (rank 2, statistically tied with the best). Each person judges a sequence by how close its switch rate is to their own ideal rate, and long streaks count against it.\n\n**The one change:** sensitivity to the distance from a person's ideal rate now grows with sequence length. It is multiplied by (transitions / 7) raised to a fitted power κ, with the prior Normal(0.5, 1). The idea is that people's sense of how often a sequence switches builds up over the flips they see. So the same departure from their ideal rate counts for more in an 8-flip sequence than in a 3-flip one.\n\n**Why:** the critique's one discrepancy that survives the FDR correction (`alternation_pref_by_length_slope`, q = 0.048) is that people's preference for the sequence with more switches falls off with length less than the incumbent predicts. A model whose sensitivity to the switch rate is the same at every length makes that preference shrink as sequences get longer. Letting sensitivity grow with length can close that gap. Keeping the parent's streak penalty also speaks to the second critique, that long streaks are under-penalised, though that one doesn't survive the FDR correction.\n\n**Files** (in `iter_2/candidate_5/`):\n- `hypothesis.md` names the parent model and the change.\n- `model_name.txt` is `length_scaled_alternation_ideal_streak`.\n- `candidate.py`